cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite the paper below."
title: "Equal ranking quality, different decisions: presentation dependence in batched scoring"
abstract: >-
  Equal ranking quality does not imply equal decisions. At a fixed threshold,
  reordering the same candidates changes which documents a scorer retains, what
  a frozen reader answers, and which response a preference model selects. This
  is strongest where several candidates share one prompt, which conditions each
  score on the presentation. Five trained scorers within 0.010 nDCG@10 span
  0.66-0.84 in retained-set overlap under reshuffling. We separate presentation
  dependence into channels with different remedies: contiguous chunking of a
  rank-sorted pool costs 0.052 nDCG@10 that round-robin assignment recovers at
  no additional cost, the answer skeleton's placeholder changes ranking quality,
  and the order channel survives both. Among training objectives that keep a
  single serving presentation, a two-view consistency penalty reaches order
  instability 0.083 against 0.128-0.130 for three matched mitigations at equal
  quality, and stays below order-averaged distillation on eleven dense bases
  without averaging at inference. Serving width improves quality only where the
  alternatives are informative. Order stability is therefore a property of a
  scorer's decisions, not only its ranking.
